                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi></head>
                <p>At his news conference of May 5, 1954 (10:30 a.m.), President Eisenhower read a
                    statement regarding Indochina and the Geneva Conference. He said that the
                    Indochina phase of the conference was in the process of being organized and the
                    issues had not yet been clarified. A large measure of initiative rested with
                    France and the Associated States, the countries most directly concerned.
                    Meanwhile, plans were proceeding for the realization of a Southeast Asia
                    security arrangement. It had never been expected, the President continued, that
                    this system could be created overnight, but the fact that such an organization
                    was in the process of formation could have an important effect upon events at
                    Geneva.</p>
                <p>For the record of the President’s news conference, including the text of his
                    statement, see <hi rendition="#italic">Public Papers of the Presidents of the
                        United States: Dwight D. Eisenhower, 1954</hi>, pages 450–459.</p>
            </div>
